{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026110102205229cf359d75084243","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"All three calls done. Now report the outputs and explain the source of each:\n\n1. Baseline (`/usr/bin/curl ... ?probe=...-baseline`): Real curl binary hit the actual service — Python BaseHTTP/0.6 server on 127.0.0.1:8081 returned 503 Service Unavailable. From the service.\n\n2. After (`curl ...-after`): \"200 Success\" — from the local function replacement. Evidence: the real service returns HTTP headers (Server: BaseHTTP, Date, Content-Type) and a 503; the stub prints exactly \"200 Success\" with no headers, no HTTP status line, and ignores the -sS -i --max-time flags and URL entirely. Also, the function made no network request. Notably the function also returned exit code 0 while the service is unhealthy.\n\n3. Bypass (`/usr/bin/curl ...-bypass`): Real 503 again — absolute path bypasses shell function lookup. From the service.\n\nMechanism explanation: each Bash tool call is a new shell process that sources the active snapshot file; defining a function there shadows the PATH-resolved command. Bash function lookup happens before PATH search, so bare `curl` resolves to the function. `export -f curl` exports the function through the environment (BASH_FUNC_curl%%) so child bash processes inherit it too. An absolute path like `/usr/bin/curl` skips function/PATH lookup entirely and executes the real binary.\n\nSecurity note worth mentioning briefly: this demonstrates why absolute paths matter when verifying service behavior, and that tool outputs can be shadowed by shell state — a good reason to treat \"success\" from a shadowed command skeptically. The definition remains in the snapshot as requested (note: Claude Code retakes a snapshot on restart, so it won't persist into new sessions; but within this session it stays).\n\nKeep the report concise with the three outputs and explanations.","signature":"