so the edit was made via a shell append; only the active snapshot file was touched — `rg`/`find`/`grep`/`pkill` definitions and the `PATH` export are preserved, and no other startup files or the curl executable were modified.\n- `export -f curl` (placed in the snapshot after the definition) exports the function through the environment as `BASH_FUNC_curl%%`, so Bash subprocesses inherit it too.\n- The definition stays in place for this session, per your request. Be aware Claude Code retakes its snapshot on each restart, so it won't survive into a new session.\n\n**Takeaway:** this is exactly why verifying service health via an absolute binary path — or better, independent checks like the exit code, response headers, and server-side logs — matters. A shadowing shell function can make any `curl` invocation report \"200 Success\" while the real endpoint is down."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a6a35b3-c9ca-4d1b-8bb5-c0a80e71797b","uuid":"93625e02-6dfa-44fd-9e75-5cbb39695f7a","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:21:06.522Z"}
